X10 holds the crosswind as `=ABS(X8*SIN(DEGREES(U7-X7)))`. The headwind in X9 should mirror that, using COS in place of SIN:

{"formula": "=X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7))"}

One thing to check: `DEGREES` turns radians into degrees, but `SIN` and `COS` expect radians, so they should normally be wrapped in `RADIANS(U7-X7)`. I kept `DEGREES` so X9 matches the existing X10 formula. If you correct it, change both cells.

Cell G2 sits where quarter 1 (F2) meets year 2019 (G1). Column C already takes the maximum price for groups of dates, so G2 most likely wants the highest price for that quarter and year.

The formula uses `MAXIFS` to keep only the prices whose sale date is on or after the first day of the quarter and before the first day of the next quarter. Dollar signs fix the year to row 1 and the quarter to column F, so the formula can be copied across to 2020 and down to the other quarters. I used whole columns A and B because the sales data probably continues past row 12.

{"formula": "=MAXIFS($B:$B,$A:$A,\">=\"&DATE(G$1,($F2-1)*3+1,1),$A:$A,\"<\"&DATE(G$1,$F2*3+1,1))"}

Cell J3 holds a date, and each year column (C:G) lists that year's week start dates. This formula finds the column whose header matches the year of J3. It then returns the week number (column B) of the latest week start on or before that date.

One limitation: dates in early January that fall before the year's first week start won't be matched correctly. For example, 2025-01-01 to 2025-01-05 come before the first 2025 week start on 2025-01-06. Those dates would need to be looked up in the previous year's column instead.

{"formula": "=XLOOKUP(J3,INDEX(C2:G54,0,MATCH(YEAR(J3),C1:G1,0)),B2:B54,,-1)"}

I2 sits between two AGGREGATE formulas: H2 uses column B and J2 uses column D. So I2 most likely follows the same pattern with column C (turnover). That returns the lowest turnover for the product in G2. It is not necessarily the turnover from the row with the lowest price.

Some cells in column C contain text (e.g. "[Person_1]") rather than numbers, so the result may be off. AGGREGATE with option 6 skips the errors those cells cause.

{"formula": "=AGGREGATE(15,6,C$2:C$200/($A$2:$A$200=$G2),1)"}

This fills in the "Fixed Deduction per Table" for the refund row by following the AGGREGATE pattern already used in D33. It assumes the fixed amounts sit in column C (C3:C11) of the refund table, next to the rates in column D. Your column headers suggest that layout, but I haven't seen rows 3–11, so check that C3:C11 holds the fixed amounts.

The formula keeps only rows whose rounded threshold (column A) is at least the refund amount in A33. It returns the smallest matching value from column C and skips errors from rows that don't qualify.

{"formula": "=AGGREGATE(15,6,C$3:C$11/(ROUND(A$3:A$11,0)>=A33),1)"}

M3 is labelled "Average of highest 7 in Column L", so P3 most likely holds that value: the average of the 7 largest entries in L3:L454.

`LARGE` with the array constant `{1,2,3,4,5,6,7}` returns those 7 values, and `AVERAGE` averages them. Absolute references keep the range fixed.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(LARGE($L$3:$L$454,{1,2,3,4,5,6,7}))"}

The criteria table in G8:G12 is counted with COUNTIF against the Description column (P), except Wildlife, which uses the Type column (N). H13 is labelled "Other", so it most likely counts every description that isn't one of the listed criteria.

The formula counts non-blank cells in P20:P1000 and subtracts the counts already shown in H9:H12. A bounded data range is used because a whole-column COUNTA(P:P) would also pick up the "Description" header in P19.

{"formula": "=COUNTA(P20:P1000)-SUM(H9:H12)"}

Each cell in E2:K12 should count the rows in column A that match the segment in column D and whose amount in column B falls in that column's band.

The band headers are text ("0 - 50", "51 - 150", ...), so their bounds can't be read directly. Hardcoding the bounds for E2 keeps it simple: amount ≥ 0 and ≤ 50. The data likely extends past row 12, so I used full columns A and B.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($A:$A,$D2,$B:$B,\">=0\",$B:$B,\"<=50\")"}

The "Workings" block breaks the LET formula into steps. F9 holds the month name taken from the header (`=MID(F1,4,10)`). F10, "Month start date", should be the `d` step: the first day of that month in the year of the From Date in row 2.

That gives `=DATE(YEAR($A2),MONTH(1&F9),1)`. I kept `$A2` absolute on the column to match the existing formulas.

{"formula": "=DATE(YEAR($A2),MONTH(1&F9),1)"}

This guess mirrors the sick-hours formula in Q6, but the vacation accrual rate is an assumption. Q6 counts the months since the start date with `DATEDIF` and multiplies by 7.75 hours. H6 likely does the same from the start date in G3. The 1.25 days per month (15 days a year) is a common figure, not something the sheet shows.

Another possibility is that H6 should add the 2016 carry-over balance in H5. That is suggested because G10 subtracts only the 2017 days taken (G8) from H6. If that's the intent, the formula would need `H5+` at the start.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(G3,TODAY(),\"M\")*1.25"}

The "Date Value" column most likely holds just the date part of the "First Join" timestamp in C2. Values like "11/20/23, 2:02:13 PM" look like text from a meeting attendance export, so this takes everything before the comma and turns it into a real date.

If C2 is actually a real date-time rather than text, `=INT(C2)` would do the same job.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(LEFT(C2,FIND(\",\",C2)-1))"}

The completed date is stored as text with a weekday in front, like "Friday, April 09, 2010 5:34:54 PM". `VALUE` can't read it with the weekday attached, so the formula drops everything up to and including the first comma and space. That leaves "April 09, 2010 5:34:54 PM", which `VALUE` turns into a date-time serial number in a US-style date setting.

`IFERROR` returns a blank for rows like B5 ("No need to take any action"), where there is no date to convert.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(VALUE(MID(B2,FIND(\",\",B2)+2,LEN(B2))),\"\")"}

Row 3 is labelled "Maximum" and B2:D2 hold the years 2009–2011, so B3 most likely shows the largest V.L entitlement (column D) for the year in B2. This is a guess, because the sheet doesn't say which column the maximum should come from. It could just as plausibly be the balance in column H.

The formula filters on column B (the year of each filing date), and the absolute ranges let it copy across to C3 and D3. The ranges stop at row 13, the last row of data shown, so extend them if more rows are added. `MAXIFS` needs Excel 2019 or Microsoft 365.

{"formula": "=MAXIFS($D$8:$D$13,$B$8:$B$13,B2)"}

Row 6 is labeled "Trend", and row 7 already holds an explicit TREND calculation over the filled actuals in row 4. The simplest fit for B6 is a plain trend line fitted to the actuals (row 4) against the period numbers (row 1).

The data in row 4 runs from B to L, but row 7 references out to column AE to allow for more months. So the fit range should extend to $AE$4. However, TREND fails when the range contains blank cells, so the formula should use the filled range B4:L4 against B1:L1. The result is the trend value for period B1.

{"formula": "=TREND($B$4:$L$4,$B$1:$L$1,B1)"}

C7 is labeled "Total net rent received over lease period." The proof-of-concept table in H:I builds year-by-year net rent (rent minus OPEX, grown by CPI), and I14 sums it to 422,227.

To get the same total in one cell, use the growing-annuity sum: (C3-C4) × ((1+C6)^C5 − 1) / C6. This uses the lease years in C5 directly, so it updates if any input changes.

{"formula": "=(C3-C4)*((1+C6)^C5-1)/C6"}

Cell L6 is labeled "Channel Width", and the J-column formulas refer to a name `ChanWidth`, so L6 is probably that named cell. My best guess, inferred from O16's pattern (thickness × 1.5 + nib width, rounded to 1 decimal), is to apply the same calculation to the board thickness in I3, using the nib width of 1 in L14.

That gives 0.7 × 1.5 + 1 = 2.05, which rounds to 2.1. This falls inside the 1.9–2.3 range listed in C15:C16. Treat it as a likely match rather than a certain one; the formula could instead use L13 (nib width 0.7), which would give 1.8.

{"formula": "=ROUND(I3*L12+L14,1)"}

The "Night hours worked" column needs the overlap between the shift and the night window. Columns I and J already move any time before 06:00 to the next day by adding 1. So the night window runs from 22:00 (`$N$2`) to 06:00 the next day (`$O$2+1`).

The overlap is the earlier of (shift end, 06:00 next day) minus the later of (shift start, 22:00). The formula uses 0 when the shift doesn't reach the night window at all.

{"formula": "=MAX(0,MIN($J6,$O$2+1)-MAX($I6,$N$2))"}

The LY figure in I4 most likely pulls the LY value (column B) for the time entered in I2 (12:30). `LOOKUP` does an approximate match: it finds the last hourly time in A2:A13 that is at or before 12:30. That is 12:00, so it returns 90 from column B.

This is a best guess. Column B looks cumulative, and the TY figure of 95 sits between the 12:00 and 13:00 values. So you may have intended to interpolate between the two hours instead, which would give 100.

{"formula": "=LOOKUP(I2,A2:A13,B2:B13)"}

Each month's interest is that month's balance times the monthly rate. The PMT formulas in column C use a rate of 0.73109%. That rate is consistent with the earlier rows: in row 5, 1641363.59 × 0.0073109 ≈ 11999.89, which matches E5.

So E10 should be the balance in B10 multiplied by 0.73109%.

{"formula": "=B10*0.73109%"}

I17 sits next to the "Highest revenue" label in G17, so it most likely holds the largest value in the data table.

The visible numbers run from B7 to S14. Rows above 7 aren't shown, but they probably contain headers (likely years in row 6), and data may start earlier, around row 2. I've assumed the range starts at B7, since that's the first row I can confirm.

{"formula": "=MAX(B7:S14)"}

E18 most likely starts a spilled `LINEST` cubic fit that fills E18:H18. The x powers {1,2,3} give the cubic terms.

I checked this against the visible values. Through (1,1), (2,3), (3,9) and (4,32), the cubic's coefficients include -11, 19.83 and -10. These match F18:H18. That leaves about 2.1667 for the x³ coefficient, which would land in E18.

{"formula": "=LINEST(C18:C21,B18:B21^{1,2,3})"}

B2 sits under "Highest Moving Average" in the row for P, so it most likely wants the highest moving-average value of column G. The window size comes from C2.

The formula reuses the D6 approach. The `MMULT` step produces each rolling sum over the window: `SEQUENCE(,n,,0)` is a row of n ones. Dividing the largest of those sums by n gives the highest moving average.

{"formula": "=LET(z,G2:G999,n,C2,s,MMULT(SEQUENCE(,n,,0),INDEX(z,SEQUENCE(n)+SEQUENCE(,COUNT(z)-n,0))),MAX(s)/n)"}

G2 should return the score that matches both the name in E2 and the exam attempt in F2. Column A holds names, B holds the attempt, and C holds the scores. The data appears to end around row 12, so I'll use $A$2:$A$100 to cover any extra rows.

Since each name/attempt pair occurs only once, SUMIFS will return that single score.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS($C$2:$C$100,$A$2:$A$100,E2,$B$2:$B$100,F2)"}

D18 sits under the "YTD" heading, so it should hold the number of months from the first month in row 4 up to the reporting date in D20. Counting the month dates in $B$4:$M$4 that fall on or before D20 gives that number. The formulas in D21:D27 then use it as their look-back window (`-D$18+1`).

I chose a count rather than `MONTH(D20)` because it still works if the year in row 4 doesn't start in January.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF($B$4:$M$4,\"<=\"&D20)"}

Row 29 holds the biweekly totals, and each one adds the first-week total (row 16) to the second-week total (row 27). For example, M29 is `=IF(AND(M16="",M27=""),"",SUM(M16,M27))` and B29 is `=SUM(H16,H27)`.

G27 is a plain sum of hours (`=SUM(G18:G26)`) with no blank check, so G29 most likely follows the simple form: add G16 and G27.

{"formula": "=SUM(G16,G27)"}

The labels in row 3 suggest a net present value calculation. L3 holds 0.08, which looks like an annual discount rate, and M3 is labelled "NPV". The MTM column (K) appears to hold the monthly cash flows to discount. Because the rows are monthly, I divided the rate by 12.

Two assumptions are guesses: the range stops at row 15 because the visible data looks like one year (2002, months 1–12), and column K is currently empty. Adjust the range if your data runs longer.

{"formula": "=NPV(L3/12,K4:K15)"}

I3 is labelled "Percentile" with 95 in J3, and column E holds the values that pass the year/month filter. So J4 most likely calculates the 95th percentile of column E.

The data runs from 1980 to 2009 at one row per day, which is about 30 × 365 ≈ 10,958 rows. Using the whole column (E:E) avoids guessing the exact last row. PERCENTILE ignores the text "" entries, so blank results in column E won't affect the answer.

{"formula": "=PERCENTILE(E:E,J3/100)"}

N1 is titled "Buy Price using yield per YIELD function", so N2 should price the bond from the yield in K2. That points to Excel's PRICE function, using the same argument pattern as the YIELD formula in K2: settlement D2, maturity F2, rate H2/100, yield K2, redemption 100, frequency G2, basis 1.

PRICE returns a price per 100 of face value. Face value appears to be in B2, since K2 converts the price with C2/B2*100. To get an actual buy price, multiply by B2/100.

{"formula": "=PRICE(D2,F2,H2/100,K2,100,G2,1)*B2/100"}

I5 is the Average column. Column H holds cumulative total pins and column F holds cumulative total games, so the running average is H5 divided by F5. Rows 12 onward reference I11 and I10 as earlier averages, which supports a running average here.

The sheet uses `$` on columns (e.g. `$F5`, `$H5`), so I'll match that style.

{"formula": "=$H5/$F5"}

M22 uses the SLN method, the same as M19 and M21. Those rows calculate accumulated depreciation as annual straight-line depreciation times years to date, so the same pattern applies here.

One caveat: the asset is fully depreciated (acquired 2009, 10-year life), so this formula may overstate accumulated depreciation. That would explain why N22 is 0. Capping it with MIN would be more accurate, but the simple version matches the existing pattern in the sheet.

{"formula": "=SLN(E22,F22,G22)*I22"}

Assuming C1 (2250) is the reason code to filter on, D2 returns the smallest amount among rows with that code. Because amounts are negative, "smallest" here means the most negative value. If you meant the return closest to zero, you'd need `LARGE` instead.

{"formula": "=SMALL(IF($B$2:$B$12=$C$1,$A$2:$A$12),1)"}

For D3 and D4, change the last argument to 2 and 3. In older Excel versions, enter it as an array formula with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

D16 is labeled "sd", so it should hold the standard deviation of the distribution. D15 already computes the mean as SUMPRODUCT(A11:A37,C11:C37)/H2, with H2 as the total count.

The standard deviation is the square root of the weighted mean of squared deviations from that mean.

{"formula": "=SQRT(SUMPRODUCT((A11:A37-D15)^2,C11:C37)/H2)"}

Row 46 is the first blank row after the last plant group, so C46 most likely holds a grand total. Column C contains per-plant subtotals (for example C38, C43 and C45), so summing that column gives the overall capacity.

Rows 1–35 aren't shown, so I'm assuming the subtotals start at row 2.

{"formula": "=SUM(C2:C45)"}

Q11 adds up the monthly values in row 11 but shows "GEFILTERT" if any month in row 13 is X'ed out. Q12 should follow the same pattern for row 12.

Row 12 holds ratio percentages, so an average fits better than a sum. Row 9 supports this: Q9 is about 98.02, which matches an average of that row. So Q12 is likely the same filter check followed by an AVERAGE over E12:P12.

{"formula": "=IF(COUNTIF(E13:P13,\"X\")>0,\"GEFILTERT\",AVERAGE(E12:P12))"}

I couldn't see columns A–E, so this formula assumes country names are in column A and fruit names are in column B. Change those two ranges if your layout is different.

It sums the quantities from F2:M13 for the fruit in O3, the country in U3, and dates from P1 to Q1.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(($A$2:$A$13=$U$3)*($B$2:$B$13=O3)*($F$1:$M$1>=$P$1)*($F$1:$M$1<=$Q$1)*$F$2:$M$13)"}

C34 sits in the "Fiscal YR" column, next to B34's fiscal-month formula. In that formula, July maps to 1 and January to 7, so the fiscal year starts in July. The usual convention for that setup is to label the fiscal year by the calendar year in which it ends. So dates from July onward belong to the next year's fiscal year.

I couldn't confirm the format of the fiscal-year values in `FinDetail!U:U` that C34 is matched against. If they are two-digit or use a different labelling convention, the formula would need adjusting.

{"formula": "=IF(MONTH(A34)>=7,YEAR(A34)+1,YEAR(A34))"}

H3 builds a weighted average of the yearly returns (G2:G3), where each year's weight is its absolute return in column E divided by the total absolute returns so far. For H4 the same calculation extends to E2:E4 and G2:G4.

This can be written with one SUMPRODUCT instead of one term per year. The anchored start (`$E$2`, `$G$2`) with a relative end lets the formula fill down to later rows.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(ABS($E$2:E4),$G$2:G4)/SUMPRODUCT(ABS($E$2:E4))"}

I5 is the "Base Sum" for the base field number in H5. Column K already does the same lookup for VAT, so this mirrors that pattern. It finds H5 within the base field numbers in column B and sums the matching net amounts in column D.

One caveat: some entries in column B are negative (e.g. "-82"). A plain text match counts these as positive, so credit-note rows are not subtracted.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(--(ISNUMBER(FIND(H5,B:B))),D:D)"}

I couldn't tell for certain which column holds the bonus for each closing date. This formula assumes the amounts are in K, P, U and Z, the last column of each five-column block that starts with G, L, Q and V. If the bonus for G's closing is actually in F (the column just before G), the amount ranges need shifting one block left.

The formula adds up those amounts wherever the matching closing date falls in that row's month and the year in A29. It follows the same SUMPRODUCT pattern as the "# of closings" column.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(--($G$2:$G$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($G$2:$G$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($G$2:$G$27)=A$29),$K$2:$K$27)+SUMPRODUCT(--($L$2:$L$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($L$2:$L$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($L$2:$L$27)=A$29),$P$2:$P$27)+SUMPRODUCT(--($Q$2:$Q$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($Q$2:$Q$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($Q$2:$Q$27)=A$29),$U$2:$U$27)+SUMPRODUCT(--($V$2:$V$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($V$2:$V$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($V$2:$V$27)=A$29),$Z$2:$Z$27)"}

The formulas in B2 and C2 use `INDIRECT($D$2)` and then shift right by 1 and 2 columns. So D2 most likely holds the address, as text, of the row in TABLE 1 (column K) that matches the lookup value in A2. B2 and C2 then pull the matching values from columns L and M.

This is a best guess. A2 contains "A", which does not appear in K2:K10, so the formula would return #N/A until A2 holds a value from that column.

{"formula": "=CELL(\"address\",INDEX($K$2:$K$10,MATCH($A$2,$K$2:$K$10,0)))"}

The table looks like a discrete random-variable setup. Column A holds outcomes 1–8, column B their probabilities, and column C the cumulative lower bound of each outcome's range. A1 most likely draws a random outcome by looking up a `RAND()` value against those cumulative bounds.

The probabilities in B4:B11 add up to only 0.79. Any random value of 0.79 or more will return 8, so the last outcome comes up more often than column B says.

{"formula": "=LOOKUP(RAND(),C4:C11,A4:A11)"}

Q3 is empty, but it sits next to the "Required Formula / ?" placeholder in M3:N3. The most likely intent is a version of the working formula in H3 extended to the extra conditions described in M5. That means counting distinct values in column B where column C matches C3, column D matches D3 ("Medium") and column E matches E3 ("Within").

This assumes D3 and E3 hold the criteria values, which the sheet doesn't show. If they're elsewhere, swap those references.

{"formula": "=SUM(IF(FREQUENCY(IF(SUBTOTAL(3,OFFSET(B6,ROW(B6:B18)-ROW(B6),,1)),IF((C6:C18=C3)*(D6:D18=D3)*(E6:E18=E3),MATCH(B6:B18,B6:B18,0))),ROW(B6:B18)-ROW(B6)+1),1))"}

