One possible reading was a lookup of the Listening score (M) in the HHR table (Q:R) or the MA table (W:X), depending on the Block (I). That doesn't fit the data: the scores in M are 10 and 15, and 10 isn't in either table (which run from 15 to 22).

{"formula": ""}

Cell I2 describes the number of low-flow pulses in the 15 days up to the date in H2, and K2 holds a check value of 2, so J2 most likely counts those pulses. Column F restarts at 1 whenever the flow category changes. Each row with F=1 and category "low" is therefore the start of a new low pulse. The formula counts those start rows whose date in column E falls in the 15 days ending on H2.

This assumes the window is "more than 15 days before H2, up to and including H2". A pulse that began before the window but runs into it is not counted.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS(E:E,\">\"&H2-15,E:E,\"<=\"&H2,D:D,\"low\",F:F,1)"}

F3 most likely needs the easting of the closest old point, because E3 and G3 both look up `$F3` in column L. This formula finds the smallest squared distance from (B3, C3) to each old point in L:M and returns that point's easting.

The range `$L$3:$L$10000` is a guess, so adjust it to fit your old data. Empty rows in that range just count as points at (0,0), which are far away and won't win. In older Excel versions you may need to enter it as an array formula with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

{"formula": "=INDEX($L$3:$L$10000,MATCH(MIN(($L$3:$L$10000-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$10000-$C3)^2),($L$3:$L$10000-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$10000-$C3)^2,0))"}

U70 is the "Energy Consumption" row for the lowest-energy option. It should follow the same INDEX/MATCH pattern as the neighbouring rows, returning the minimum numeric value from U58:U65.

The closest analogue is AE72, which uses `INDEX(AE58:AE65, ...)`. Following that, U70 would be `INDEX(U58:U65, MATCH(MIN(...)))`. Since that just returns the minimum value itself, `=MIN(IF(ISNUMBER(U58:U65),U58:U65))` would give the same result. I'll stay with the INDEX pattern to match the rest of the sheet.

{"formula": "=INDEX(U58:U65,MATCH(MIN(IF(ISNUMBER(U58:U65),U58:U65)),U58:U65,0))"}

Row 21 has a formula in D21 that uses SUMIF with a combined date-ID key: `=SUMIF($D$80:$D$1000, $A21&"-"&D$2, $C$80:$D$1000)`. C21 most likely follows the same pattern, with the column-relative reference changed to C$2.

I kept the sum range exactly as D21 has it (`$C$80:$D$1000`) so the formula matches when filled across.

{"formula": "=SUMIF($D$80:$D$1000, $A21&\"-\"&C$2, $C$80:$D$1000)"}

The row 18 cells hold column totals of rows 13–17. For example, column D gives 6+6+6+6+2 = 26, and column E gives 4+2+3+2+2 = 13. So N18 should follow the same pattern.

The values appear as "26/30", which may be a number format like `#/30` rather than text. If they are actually text built by joining strings, the formula would differ.

{"formula": "=SUM(N13:N17)"}

C2 most likely needs the mileage for the trip in A2→B2. A lookup table starts at row 11 (from, to, miles), so the formula matches both the "from" and "to" columns there and returns the miles.

The visible table only shows row 12, so the range end of row 100 is an assumption. Adjust it to wherever the table actually ends. A trip listed in the reverse direction (B→A) won't match.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS($C$12:$C$100,$A$12:$A$100,A2,$B$12:$B$100,B2)"}

Row 37 is labelled "XIRR", and row 36 holds what looks like a closing value dated `=TODAY()`. So B37 most likely computes the internal rate of return for column B's cash flows against the dates in column A.

The visible data only starts at row 27, so I'm assuming the cash flows begin at row 2 under a header in row 1. Adjust the start row if your data begins elsewhere. The date range is anchored with `$` so the formula can be copied across to columns C–L.

{"formula": "=XIRR(B2:B36,$A$2:$A$36)"}
